# PART 4 — THE MOST IMPORTANT REQUIREMENT: DEPARTMENT-AWARE SCHEDULING

Understand this carefully.

CIT has multiple departments, such as:

- Computer Science and Engineering (CSE)
- Electronics and Communication Engineering (ECE)
- Electrical Engineering (EE)
- Mechanical Engineering (ME)
- Civil Engineering (CE)
- Other departments that may be added later.

The application must support an institute-wide timetable without accidentally mixing or blocking unrelated departments.

A department is an organizational and permission scope. It is NOT a universal scheduling conflict resource.

## Scenario 1 — Independent department classes

Suppose:

CSE:
- CSE Year 1 students.
- Faculty F01.
- Room A.

ECE:
- ECE Year 1 students.
- Faculty F02.
- Room B.

Both classes can happen on Monday Period 2.

There is no conflict because:
- Student groups differ.
- Faculty differ.
- Rooms differ.

The solver must allow this.

Never impose an unnecessary institute-wide rule that only one class can happen at a time.

## Scenario 2 — Shared faculty

Suppose:
- CSE has a class with Faculty F07.
- ECE also has a class with Faculty F07.
- Both are scheduled for Monday Period 3.

This is a conflict.

A faculty member is an institute-wide resource by default.

Department affiliation does not create separate faculty availability.

The solver must reject this double booking.

## Scenario 3 — Shared room

Suppose:
- CSE uses Room A.
- ECE also uses Room A.
- Both classes are scheduled simultaneously.

This is a conflict.

A physical room cannot be independently allocated by two departments at the same time.

Room occupancy must be checked globally.

## Scenario 4 — Joint CSE and ECE class

Suppose a common Mathematics lecture is attended by:

- CSE Year 1.
- ECE Year 1.

One faculty member teaches both groups in one common room.

Represent this as ONE teaching session with:

- Multiple participating student groups.
- Assigned faculty.
- One shared room.
- Combined required attendance.
- One timetable assignment.

The session consumes:
- CSE Year 1 group resource.
- ECE Year 1 group resource.
- Faculty resource.
- Room resource.

It must conflict with any simultaneous class involving either participating group, the same faculty or the same room.

Do not create two independent copies of the same joint session.

## Scenario 5 — Admin generates only the CSE timetable

Suppose Admin requests timetable generation for CSE.

The system must:
- Identify the CSE sessions.
- Identify sessions involving CSE student groups.
- Identify joint sessions involving CSE and other departments.
- Preserve unrelated ECE assignments.
- Lock unaffected assignments as solver constraints.
- Check shared faculty availability globally.
- Check shared room availability globally.
- Check all participating student groups.
- Validate the complete affected resource graph.

The system must not regenerate or erase the ECE timetable accidentally.

A joint CSE+ECE session must not be ignored simply because the generation request targets CSE.

If moving a joint session would affect a department outside the requester's authority, require appropriate Admin approval or reject the proposed change.

Never silently modify another department's schedule.

## Scenario 6 — Department Coordinator permissions

Department Coordinators must operate within their authorized department scope.

They must not:
- Modify unrelated department courses.
- Modify unrelated student groups.
- Change another department's timetable without authority.
- Move a joint cross-department session unilaterally.
- Override global faculty conflicts.
- Override room conflicts.
- Publish a global timetable without permission.

Admin has institute-wide authority.

A user may have multiple role assignments and scopes.

The official competition roles remain:

- admin
- department_coordinator
- faculty
- club

Do not invent additional competition roles.

"Club Representative" is a UI label, not a fifth role.

## Required database and solver design

Use explicit resource occupancy.

The conflict key must be based on:

- Resource type.
- Resource ID.
- Institute-local day.
- Period or time interval.
- Applicable timetable/version.

Department ID must not be used as part of the uniqueness key for physical resource occupancy.

The database must support:
- Multiple department affiliations for faculty where required.
- Multiple participating student groups per teaching session.
- Multiple faculty assignments where supported.
- Explicit department ownership for department-level events.
- Cross-department session identification.
- Generation scope.
- Locked baseline assignments.
- Full institute-wide conflict validation.

Treat source-provided student groups as schedulable conflict units unless an explicit group-overlap model is available.

Do not assume that nested groups are disjoint.

If group overlap is possible, design an explicit cohort-overlap model rather than silently scheduling the same students twice.

